================================================================================
Make the analytics job's BFS depth cap reachable so betweenness/closeness ship
================================================================================

Context
-------

Reported: in job mode betweenness and closeness come back at zero, labelled
"Not computed for this graph ... either no pivots were sampled or the
breadth-first search hit its depth cap".

Read the summary row the last run actually wrote
(benoit_cayla.ontobricks.graph_metrics_bigcustomers_1_summary): node_count
1,349,202, edge_count 1,549,860, component_count 4,986, pivot_count 64,
bfs_complete false. Pivots were sampled, so it is unambiguously the depth-cap
branch. The withholding itself is correct — a truncated BFS biases the distance
sums, and JobMetrics refuses to publish an estimate it cannot stand behind.

The defect is that the cap could not be raised. ``--max-depth`` was the one
tuning parameter never wired through: ``pagerank_iterations`` and ``pivots``
both travel Settings -> JobMetrics -> LakeflowRunner -> job_parameters and are
declared in graph_analytics.job.yml, while max_depth existed only as an
argparse default. Every run was pinned at 12 with no way out short of editing
source.

12 came from the assumption that knowledge graphs have small diameters. This
graph averages degree 2.3 — long Customer -> Order -> ... chains, not a small
world — so it needs far more levels. The BFS itself is sound: bfs_iteration
anti-joins each new level against the accumulated table, so every
(pivot, node) pair expands exactly once and the frontier is guaranteed to
empty. It simply needed more room.

Headroom is close to free because the loop breaks the moment the frontier
empties, so the cap is a runaway guard rather than a budget. Hence a generous
new default rather than a cautious bump.

Clustering at zero, also mentioned in the report, is not a defect. Clustering
is never placed in unavailable_metrics in job mode, so it renders the separate
"All values are 0 ... bipartite structure" notice, and the clustering SQL is
checked against nx.clustering on four graph shapes. Zero triangles is the
correct answer for a Customer -> Order -> Product graph. Left alone.

Changes
-------

1. src/jobs/graph_analytics_job.py
   DEFAULT_MAX_DEPTH 12 -> 32, with the rationale rewritten to say the cap is a
   runaway guard and to warn against tuning it back down on the small-diameter
   assumption. The truncation warning now reports how many (pivot, node) pairs
   were left unexplored and names the setting to raise, instead of claiming the
   frontier was "still growing" — which was not what the code measured.

2. src/shared/config/settings.py
   New ``analytics_job_max_depth`` (ONTOBRICKS_ANALYTICS_JOB_MAX_DEPTH),
   default 32.

3. src/back/core/graph_analysis/LakeflowRunner.py
   ``submit`` / ``run_and_wait`` take max_depth and send it as the ``max_depth``
   job parameter.

4. src/back/core/graph_analysis/JobMetrics.py
   Takes max_depth and forwards it to the runner. Module docstring corrected:
   it claimed betweenness and closeness were unavailable in this mode, which
   stopped being true when pivot sampling landed.

5. src/back/objects/digitaltwin/DigitalTwin.py
   Reads the setting when building JobMetrics.

6. resources/graph_analytics.job.yml
   Declares the ``max_depth`` parameter (default 32) and passes ``--max-depth``
   to the task.

7. tests/units/core/test_lakeflow_runner.py
   max_depth reaches job_parameters as a string.

8. tests/units/core/test_job_metrics.py
   max_depth reaches the runner; helper gained the argument.

9. tests/units/core/test_graph_analytics_job_sql.py
   A 25-hop path completes under the default cap (it would not have under 12);
   a star still stops after its own depth, proving the headroom costs nothing.

Deployment note
---------------

The app now sends a job parameter the deployed job does not yet declare, and
``run_now`` rejects unknown parameters. The job must be redeployed (make
deploy) before the next analysis run, otherwise it fails at submit.

